    mostLikelyDiagnoses:
      'An RAPD found with a swollen, hyperemic disc and pain on eye movement in a younger patient points to optic neuritis. An RAPD with a pale, swollen disc and altitudinal field loss in an older patient with vascular risk factors points to NAION, and the same picture with systemic symptoms (headache, jaw claudication, scalp tenderness) must be evaluated for arteritic AION from giant cell arteritis. An RAPD with a pale, edematous retina and cherry-red spot indicates a prior or acute central retinal artery occlusion. An RAPD found together with an extensive retinal detachment or large area of retinal ischemia reflects the extent of the outer/inner retinal damage rather than isolated optic nerve disease. An RAPD with a normal-appearing fundus and no other ocular explanation, especially with a temporal visual field defect suggesting chiasmal involvement, raises concern for a compressive lesion along the anterior visual pathway and requires neuroimaging. Markedly asymmetric glaucomatous cupping can also produce a measurable RAPD proportional to the degree of optic nerve fiber loss.',
