    mostLikelyDiagnoses:
      'Chronic bilateral burning and fluctuating vision with a low tear meniscus, reduced tear break-up time, and interpalpebral punctate staining most commonly reflects dry eye disease, whether aqueous-deficient (low Schirmer values), evaporative (meibomian gland dysfunction predominant), or a mixed picture, which is the most common presentation overall. Prominent lid margin thickening, telangiectasia, collarettes at the lash base, and poor meibomian gland expression with a foamy tear film points to blepharitis/meibomian gland dysfunction as the primary evaporative driver. Dryness with a documented poor or incomplete blink, lagophthalmos, or facial nerve weakness suggests exposure keratopathy layered on top of or instead of primary dry eye. Reduced or absent corneal sensation with variable staining severity that seems disproportionate to reported symptoms suggests neurotrophic keratopathy, often following herpetic disease, refractive surgery, or diabetic neuropathy. Redundant, folded superior bulbar conjunctiva with superior corneal/conjunctival staining and a positive lid-pinch sign suggests superior limbic keratoconjunctivitis. Recurrent episodes of sharp pain, particularly on waking, in a patient with an underlying dry eye/epithelial basement membrane picture suggests recurrent corneal erosion.',
