    managementConsiderations:
      'The great majority of dry eye disease is managed routinely in primary eye care with a stepwise approach: patient education and environmental modification, artificial tears/lubricants, warm compresses and lid hygiene for meibomian gland dysfunction, and escalation to anti-inflammatory therapy (topical cyclosporine, lifitegrast, or short-course topical corticosteroid) or punctal occlusion for more significant or aqueous-deficient disease. In-office procedures (thermal meibomian gland expression, intense pulsed light) are options for refractory evaporative disease. Suspected Sjögren syndrome or another underlying autoimmune disease warrants referral for systemic workup (e.g., SSA/SSB antibodies, rheumatology referral) alongside ongoing ocular surface management. Filamentary keratitis, non-healing epithelial defects, corneal thinning, or a significantly compromised ocular surface should prompt more urgent corneal evaluation, since these carry risk of infection, scarring, or perforation if left unmanaged. Neurotrophic or exposure-related components change management priorities toward protecting the ocular surface (e.g., moisture chamber, lubricating ointment, possible tarsorrhaphy) rather than symptom-directed dry eye therapy alone.',
