    mostLikelyDiagnoses:
      'A visible superficial corneal or conjunctival foreign body with corresponding fluorescein staining and a clear history of a foreign-body event is the most straightforward cause and is confirmed on slit-lamp exam. Persistent foreign-body sensation with no visible corneal lesion but linear vertical corneal staining should prompt upper lid eversion to find a retained subtarsal foreign body. An epithelial defect with fluorescein staining and no retained foreign body, following a known scratch or contact lens-related trauma, indicates a simple corneal abrasion. A history of high-velocity metal-on-metal work with a small, easily missed corneal or scleral entry wound raises concern for a penetrating injury with an intraocular foreign body, which requires a much more thorough workup than the presentation alone suggests. Recurrent sharp pain on waking, often with a history of a prior corneal abrasion or an inherited corneal dystrophy, and a loose or hazy epithelium on exam, points to recurrent corneal erosion. Chronic, bilateral gritty sensation without an acute event and with reduced tear break-up time points to dry eye disease. Inward-directed lashes contacting the globe with corneal punctate staining in the area of contact indicates trichiasis.',
