      'Watery discharge with follicular conjunctival reaction, preauricular lymphadenopathy, and a recent upper respiratory illness or sick contact points to viral (typically adenoviral) conjunctivitis, the most common overall cause. Ropy, stringy mucoid discharge with itching and papillary conjunctival reaction, often bilateral and seasonal, points to allergic conjunctivitis. Thick, purulent discharge that reaccumulates quickly after wiping, with a papillary reaction, suggests bacterial conjunctivitis; when the discharge is copious and hyperacute with rapidly progressive chemosis and lid swelling, gonococcal conjunctivitis should be specifically considered given its potential for rapid corneal involvement. Chronic, low-grade mucopurulent discharge and follicular conjunctivitis, especially in a sexually active young adult or from a region where trachoma is endemic, suggests chlamydial conjunctivitis. Discharge localized to the medial canthus with a tender, swollen, warm lacrimal sac that expresses discharge on palpation points to dacryocystitis; discharge and swelling more focally along the lid margin near the punctum, with pouting of the punctum, points to canaliculitis. Chronic itching with mucous discharge and large papillae under the upper lid in a contact lens wearer suggests giant papillary conjunctivitis.',
