    mostLikelyDiagnoses:
      'Photophobia with ciliary/circumlimbal flush, a small or irregular pupil, and anterior chamber cell/flare points to anterior uveitis, one of the most common significant intraocular causes. Photophobia with pain and a corneal epithelial defect, infiltrate, or foreign body points to a corneal cause such as keratitis or abrasion, since the richly innervated cornea is a major driver of photophobia when inflamed or disrupted. Mild-to-moderate photophobia without significant redness or pain, in a patient with a compatible headache pattern, points to migraine, in which photophobia is a defining associated feature and can persist interictally. Photophobia with a normal-appearing eye but chronic ocular surface irritation and reduced tear break-up time points to dry eye disease. Photophobia with fever, headache, and neck stiffness should prompt urgent evaluation for meningitis regardless of ocular exam findings. In children, photophobia with nystagmus, iris transillumination, and fundus hypopigmentation points to albinism, while photophobia with tearing and an enlarged, hazy cornea in an infant points to congenital glaucoma.',
