      'New metamorphopsia or a central scotoma with subretinal or intraretinal fluid, hemorrhage, or a pigment epithelial detachment on OCT in an older patient, especially with prior drusen, points to exudative (wet) age-related macular degeneration and is the diagnosis that must be prioritized given the benefit of prompt treatment. Gradual, painless central acuity loss with drusen and pigmentary change but no fluid points to non-exudative (dry) AMD, including geographic atrophy in advanced cases. Mild-to-moderate metamorphopsia with a glinting or cellophane-like sheen over the macula and a visible membrane with retinal striae on OCT points to an epiretinal membrane. A central scotoma with a small, round full-thickness defect on OCT points to a macular hole. Sudden distortion or micropsia with a serous, dome-shaped macular elevation on OCT — often in a younger patient with a stress or corticosteroid history — points to central serous chorioretinopathy. Distortion or blur with macular thickening, cystic spaces, hemorrhages, and exudates in a patient with diabetes points to diabetic macular edema.',
