      'A mild burn with a partial epithelial defect, clear corneal stroma, and no limbal ischemia is Roper-Hall grade I and carries an excellent prognosis. Grade II adds mild corneal haze with limbal ischemia affecting less than one-third of the circumference. Grade III shows a total epithelial defect, stromal haze obscuring iris detail, and one- to two-thirds limbal ischemia — visual prognosis worsens significantly at this stage. Grade IV (Roper-Hall) involves opaque cornea with obscured view of the iris/pupil and greater than two-thirds limbal ischemia, historically associated with a poor prognosis; the Dua classification subdivides this most severe range further (grades IV-VI, based on clock hours of limbal involvement combined with the percentage of conjunctival involvement) because outcomes vary widely across it and total limbal/conjunctival involvement carries the worst prognosis. Thermal burns typically cause more localized, full-thickness injury at the point of contact without the same diffuse penetration seen with alkali chemicals, but can still be visually devastating if central and full-thickness. Progressive limbal stem cell deficiency, with a whorled, vascularized, and unstable corneal epithelium, is the characteristic late complication of a severe burn once the acute injury has healed.',
