    mostLikelyDiagnoses:
      'A white, often chalky or calcified-appearing mass on dilated exam and B-scan, with or without associated retinal detachment, vitreous seeding, or a family history of the disease, is retinoblastoma — the diagnosis that must be excluded first in any child with leukocoria regardless of how benign the eye otherwise appears. A dense, central white opacity confined to the lens with an otherwise normal-appearing posterior segment (when visualized) suggests congenital cataract, which itself requires urgent surgical timing counseling to prevent deprivation amblyopia. A unilateral white reflex with a retrolental fibrovascular stalk, a small eye (microphthalmia), and a shallow anterior chamber, often without prematurity, suggests persistent fetal vasculature. Yellow-white subretinal exudation with dilated, telangiectatic retinal vessels and no calcification, typically in a young boy, points toward Coats disease. A history of prematurity, low birth weight, and supplemental oxygen exposure with peripheral retinal vascular abnormalities and possible tractional retinal detachment points to advanced retinopathy of prematurity. A white pupil with vitritis, a localized granuloma, or vitreoretinal traction bands in a child with cat/soil exposure suggests ocular toxocariasis.',
