    mostLikelyDiagnoses:
      'A pupil-involving CN III palsy — ptosis, an eye deviated down and out with limited adduction/elevation/depression, and a dilated, poorly reactive pupil — is presumed to be caused by a compressive intracranial aneurysm (classically at the posterior communicating artery) until neuroimaging proves otherwise, since aneurysmal compression preferentially affects the superficially located parasympathetic pupillary fibers before the deeper motor fascicles. A pupil-sparing, complete CN III palsy in an older patient with vascular risk factors (diabetes, hypertension) is more typically a microvascular ischemic mononeuropathy, though it still warrants close monitoring for evolving pupil involvement in the first 5-7 days, since aneurysms can occasionally present with an initially spared pupil. Vertical/oblique diplopia worse in downgaze and on head tilt toward the affected side, with excyclotorsion and often a compensatory head tilt to the opposite shoulder, points to a CN IV (trochlear) palsy — the most common cause of an isolated vertical diplopia and, when longstanding on old photographs, frequently a congenital palsy that has recently decompensated rather than a new acute lesion. Horizontal diplopia worse at distance with an isolated inability to abduct one eye points to a CN VI (abducens) palsy, commonly microvascular in older adults with vascular risk factors but also seen with raised intracranial pressure (given the nerve\'s long intracranial course) or, in a child, as an early sign of a pontine glioma. Fluctuating ptosis and diplopia that worsen with fatigue or through the day, without pupil involvement, suggest ocular myasthenia gravis masquerading as a cranial nerve palsy — myasthenia never affects the pupil, which helps distinguish it from CN III palsy.',
