    mostLikelyDiagnoses:
      'Pain, redness, decreased vision, and hypopyon developing within the first 1-2 weeks after cataract surgery is presumed acute postoperative bacterial endophthalmitis until vitreous tap and culture prove otherwise — this is the diagnosis that must be excluded first and most urgently in any postoperative patient with these features, since delay materially worsens visual outcome. Diffuse corneal edema and severe anterior chamber reaction beginning within the first 12-48 hours, often with a limbus-to-limbus distribution and, notably, without the vitreous involvement typical of infection, and sometimes clustering among multiple patients operated on the same day, points to toxic anterior segment syndrome (TASS), a sterile inflammatory reaction to a contaminant introduced during surgery rather than a true infection. A visible white or off-white cortical lens fragment in the anterior chamber or vitreous, often with elevated IOP and low-grade inflammation, indicates retained lens material from an intraoperative posterior capsule rupture. Painless, gradual central vision decline weeks to months after otherwise uncomplicated surgery, with cystic intraretinal fluid on OCT, indicates cystoid macular edema (Irvine-Gass syndrome). Gradual, painless blurring of vision months to years after surgery, improved with Nd:YAG capsulotomy, indicates posterior capsule opacification, the most common long-term complication of cataract surgery. A decentered or tilted IOL, monocular diplopia, or a sudden refractive shift, particularly in an eye with zonular risk factors, indicates IOL subluxation or dislocation.',
    urgency: 'Emergency',
    managementConsiderations:
      'Any postoperative cataract patient with pain, redness, and decreased vision within the first two weeks after surgery should be treated as a same-day ophthalmic emergency until endophthalmitis is excluded, since even a several-hour delay in intravitreal antibiotic therapy can significantly worsen the visual outcome — this requires emergent same-day referral to the operating surgeon or a retina specialist for vitreous/aqueous tap and culture plus intravitreal antibiotics, with vitrectomy considered for eyes presenting with light-perception-only vision. Suspected TASS is managed with aggressive topical corticosteroids rather than antibiotics once infection is felt unlikely, and prompts the surgical center to investigate and correct the underlying contamination source (instrument reprocessing, solution, or intraocular medication issue) to prevent further cases. A retained lens fragment generally requires prompt (typically within 1-2 weeks) referral to a retina specialist for pars plana vitrectomy and lensectomy, since retained cortical or nuclear material incites persistent inflammation and can cause secondary glaucoma if left in place. Cystoid macular edema is managed with topical NSAIDs and corticosteroids, escalating to periocular or intravitreal therapy for refractory cases, on a prompt-to-routine timeline since it is not typically vision-threatening in the acute sense. Posterior capsule opacification is managed electively with Nd:YAG laser capsulotomy once visually significant. A dislocated or significantly decentered IOL warrants surgical referral for repositioning, suture/scleral fixation, or exchange, urgency depending on the degree of visual disruption and whether the lens is threatening to prolapse into the vitreous.',
