    mostLikelyDiagnoses:
      'No visible retinopathy on a well-performed dilated exam in a patient with good glycemic control indicates no diabetic retinopathy, though annual re-screening remains indicated given cumulative lifetime risk. Microaneurysms only, without other findings, indicates mild nonproliferative diabetic retinopathy (NPDR), the most common finding in patients with more than a few years of diabetes duration. More extensive findings — dot-blot hemorrhages, hard exudates, cotton-wool spots — that fall short of the severe NPDR criteria indicate moderate NPDR. Meeting any one of the "4-2-1 rule" criteria (hemorrhages in all four quadrants, venous beading in two or more quadrants, or IRMA in at least one quadrant), without frank neovascularization, indicates severe NPDR, which carries a substantial one-year risk of progressing to proliferative disease. Neovascularization of the disc or elsewhere, with or without vitreous or preretinal hemorrhage, indicates proliferative diabetic retinopathy (PDR), the sight-threatening stage at which panretinal photocoagulation or anti-VEGF therapy is considered. Central or paracentral retinal thickening with cystic change on OCT, at any retinopathy severity level, indicates diabetic macular edema, which is graded and treated independently of the NPDR/PDR retinopathy stage and is treated urgently when center-involving.',
    urgency: 'Routine',
    managementConsiderations:
      'The diabetic eye exam itself is a routine, scheduled screening encounter, but the recommended follow-up interval and any escalation are driven entirely by exam findings using standard, evidence-based intervals: no retinopathy or mild NPDR is followed annually; moderate NPDR is followed every 6-12 months; severe (and moderately severe) NPDR is followed every 2-4 months given the high risk of progression to PDR within a year; and any diabetic macular edema is followed every 4-6 months if non-center-involving or referred promptly (often within days to a couple of weeks) to retina for center-involving edema, which is typically treated with intravitreal anti-VEGF therapy. Proliferative diabetic retinopathy, vitreous hemorrhage, tractional retinal detachment, or neovascular glaucoma found on an otherwise routine screening exam converts this from a routine visit to an urgent-to-emergent referral to retina, since panretinal photocoagulation and/or anti-VEGF therapy and, for tractional detachment, vitrectomy, are time-sensitive to prevent further vision loss. All diabetic patients, regardless of retinopathy status, should be counseled on the central importance of glycemic, blood pressure, and lipid control in slowing retinopathy progression, and pregnant patients with pre-existing diabetes require closer-interval monitoring throughout pregnancy.',
