    mostLikelyDiagnoses:
      "This is a symptom-triage entry point rather than a single diagnosis: the pattern of findings on the battery above localizes the cause before a specific workup is pursued. A receded near point of convergence with near-greater-than-distance exophoria and reduced positive fusional vergence points toward convergence insufficiency. Near-greater-than-distance esophoria with a high AC/A ratio points toward convergence excess. Reduced amplitude of accommodation and facility without a significant phoria points toward an accommodative disorder (accommodative insufficiency, excess, or infacility). Symptoms that resolve once uncorrected or under-corrected refractive error — including early presbyopia — is addressed point to a simple refractive cause. Fluctuating blur that clears transiently with blinking, with reduced tear break-up time and surface staining, points to dry eye disease exacerbated by reduced blink rate during near/screen tasks (often compounding, rather than replacing, a true accommodative or vergence problem). Each of these patterns is then confirmed and managed using the corresponding dedicated workup.",
    urgency: 'Routine',
    managementConsiderations:
      "Uncorrected or outdated refractive error should be addressed first, since it is the most common remediable cause of near-specific symptoms and can confound accommodative/vergence testing if left uncorrected. Once refractive error is optimized, findings are triaged to the appropriate specific pathway — convergence insufficiency, convergence excess, accommodative insufficiency, accommodative excess/infacility, or decompensated phoria — for detailed workup and evidence-based treatment (typically office-based vision therapy per the CITT protocols, with or without a reading add or prism). Ocular surface disease contributing to near-task symptoms is treated with standard dry eye management (lubrication, blink exercises, environmental modification) alongside any BV-directed treatment. Any red-flag feature identified during this triage should be redirected to the diplopia, headache, or new-onset strabismus workups rather than managed as benign asthenopia.",
