    mostLikelyDiagnoses:
      "An esophoria or intermittent esotropia that is significantly greater at near than at distance, with a high calculated or gradient AC/A ratio and a normal near point of convergence, in a patient without significant uncorrected hyperopia, indicates non-strabismic (or minimally strabismic) convergence excess. When a manifest near esotropia is present and it substantially reduces or resolves with full cycloplegic hyperopic correction in place, the diagnosis is instead (fully or partially) refractive accommodative esotropia; when the esotropia persists at near despite full hyperopic correction but improves further with an additional plus reading add, it reflects the high-AC/A subtype of accommodative esotropia, which convergence excess overlaps with substantially in presentation and management. Reduced acuity in the more frequently deviating or more hyperopic eye should raise concern for coexisting amblyopia, which is common with accommodative esotropia and changes the urgency and sequencing of treatment.",
    urgency: 'Routine',
    managementConsiderations:
      "Full cycloplegic refraction and correction of any hyperopia is the essential first step, since it directly reduces the accommodative drive to convergence and often substantially reduces or eliminates the near deviation on its own. If a symptomatic or strabismic near esodeviation persists despite full spectacle correction, an executive or flat-top bifocal (plus add for near) is commonly prescribed to further reduce accommodative demand at near, particularly for a high-AC/A pattern; the add power is typically the minimum needed to neutralize the near deviation, reassessed periodically as the child grows since many high-AC/A esotropias improve with age. Vision therapy targeting negative fusional vergence can help reduce reliance on the bifocal in select patients. Any coexisting amblyopia should be treated with standard refractive correction and, if needed, occlusion or penalization therapy, sequenced before or alongside strabismus management. A constant, non-improving esotropia despite adequate optical management may require strabismus surgery evaluation. Acute new-onset esotropia in an older patient should follow the diplopia/new-onset strabismus workup rather than this pathway.",
