    mostLikelyDiagnoses:
      "A stable-magnitude phoria with newly reduced fusional vergence reserves that no longer satisfy Sheard's or Percival's criterion indicates decompensation of a previously well-controlled heterophoria — commonly precipitated by illness, fatigue, a new spectacle correction, or age-related reduction in fusional amplitudes — rather than a new structural or neurologic deviation. Decompensation of a near exophoria follows a convergence-insufficiency-like pattern; decompensation of a near esophoria follows a convergence-excess-like pattern; decompensation of a distance phoria (more often exophoric) suggests a divergence weakness pattern. A genuinely new deviation, a change in the phoria's magnitude compared to prior records, or true incomitance should instead be worked up as new-onset strabismus or diplopia rather than assumed to be benign decompensation.",
