    mostLikelyDiagnoses:
      "It is important to state plainly that \"vertical heterophoria\" as a distinct clinical syndrome linking small vertical phorias to chronic dizziness, disequilibrium, and diffuse head/neck symptoms — sometimes marketed as \"binocular vision dysfunction\" — is not a universally accepted diagnostic entity. It is described primarily in optometric case series and a limited, largely uncontrolled literature; mainstream ophthalmology and neurology have not endorsed prism treatment of small, comitant vertical phorias as an established therapy for dizziness or vestibular-type symptoms, and no AAO or AAN practice guideline currently recognizes it as a defined syndrome, in clear contrast to the well-replicated, randomized-trial evidence base supporting convergence insufficiency. When a small vertical phoria found on cover testing is comitant, stable over time, symptomatic specifically with sustained visual tasks rather than primarily vestibular in character, and demonstrably and reproducibly improved with a low-power trial of vertical prism or vergence therapy, it is reasonable to manage it as a decompensated vertical phoria contributing to the patient's asthenopia. Before attributing dizziness, disequilibrium, or chronic headache primarily to a small phoria, an incomitant deviation (CN IV palsy, skew deviation from a brainstem or cerebellar lesion), thyroid eye disease, and myasthenia gravis should be reasonably excluded.",
    urgency: 'Routine',
    managementConsiderations:
      "Given the genuine absence of an objective gold-standard diagnostic test and the limited, non-consensus evidence base, treatment is guided primarily by reproducible symptom response to a conservative trial rather than by the presence of the phoria alone. A trial of low-amount vertical prism (Fresnel or ground-in), calculated from the measured phoria and titrated to the minimum amount producing clear, repeatable symptomatic benefit, and/or vertical vergence therapy, can be offered when a comitant vertical phoria correlates well with visually-triggered symptoms. Expectations should be set honestly with the patient given the limited high-quality trial evidence for this specific presentation, in contrast to the CITT-established evidence base for convergence insufficiency. Co-management with vestibular therapy, neurology, or otolaryngology is often appropriate when vestibular-type symptoms (dizziness, disequilibrium) predominate, since these overlap substantially with recognized vestibular, migrainous, and post-concussive conditions that should not be overlooked in favor of a prism-only approach. Any incomitant, progressive, or truly diplopic presentation should be escalated through the diplopia and new-onset strabismus workups rather than managed through this pathway.",
