    mostLikelyDiagnoses:
      "The eye examination should identify and treat any genuine, testable optometric contributor to reading difficulty — uncorrected or asymmetric refractive error, amblyopia, strabismus, or a diagnosable accommodative/vergence disorder such as convergence insufficiency — since these are well-documented causes of eyestrain, blur, and reduced reading endurance and are treatable with glasses, patching, or evidence-based office-based vergence/accommodative therapy. However, reduced reading fluency, letter reversals, poor phonological awareness, and comprehension difficulty despite normal visual acuity and a normal binocular vision examination most often reflect dyslexia or another primary language-based learning disability rather than an optometric problem. The joint position of the American Academy of Ophthalmology, American Academy of Pediatrics, and American Association for Pediatric Ophthalmology and Strabismus (2009, reaffirmed 2014) is that there is no adequate scientific evidence that subtle eye or visual problems cause dyslexia, or that vision therapy, tinted lenses/filters, or eye exercises are effective treatments for dyslexia or other primary learning disabilities. This position is not shared by some optometric organizations and behavioral/developmental optometrists, who maintain that undiagnosed, treatable binocular vision or accommodative dysfunction is a genuine, underrecognized contributing (though not causal) factor for some struggling readers — this is a real, ongoing point of disagreement between professional organizations and should be represented as such rather than as settled either way. Optometric testing can reliably identify and treat a coexisting, measurable binocular vision or accommodative disorder, and can rule out uncorrected refractive error or ocular pathology as a contributor; it cannot diagnose dyslexia or any other specific learning disability, which requires formal psychoeducational and/or speech-language evaluation.",
