    mostLikelyDiagnoses:
      "This entry is deliberately a triage tool rather than a single diagnosis: the pattern of findings on the battery above localizes the problem before a specific-entity workup is pursued. A receded near point of convergence with near-greater-than-distance exophoria and reduced positive fusional vergence points toward convergence insufficiency. Near-greater-than-distance esophoria with a high AC/A ratio points toward convergence excess (or, in a child with hyperopia, accommodative esotropia). An exodeviation greater at distance than near suggests a divergence-excess pattern. An esodeviation greater at distance than near suggests divergence insufficiency, which — if acute or acquired in an adult — must also be differentiated from a CN VI palsy or, rarely, divergence paralysis before being treated as benign. Reduced accommodative amplitude or facility without a significant phoria points toward a primary accommodative disorder. Symptoms that resolve once refractive correction is optimized, with otherwise normal vergence and accommodative findings, point back to simple uncorrected refractive error. A previously documented, stable phoria that has newly become symptomatic points toward decompensated phoria. Each localized pattern should then be confirmed and managed using its corresponding dedicated workup.",
