    mostLikelyDiagnoses:
      "Reduced best-corrected acuity in one eye with a history of constant unilateral strabismus (most often esotropia) and no structural fundus abnormality indicates strabismic amblyopia, the most common form. Reduced unilateral acuity with a significant interocular difference in refractive error and straight eyes indicates anisometropic amblyopia, which can be subtle and go undetected without a vision or refraction screening since the eyes appear cosmetically normal. Bilaterally reduced acuity with high, roughly symmetric, uncorrected refractive error in both eyes indicates isoametropic (ametropic) amblyopia. Significant uncorrected astigmatism causing a chronically blurred meridional retinal image, whether unilateral or bilateral, produces meridional (astigmatic) amblyopia. Reduced acuity in an eye with a visually significant structural opacity present from infancy or early childhood — congenital cataract, corneal opacity, or a ptotic lid occluding the visual axis — indicates deprivation amblyopia, the least common form but potentially the most severe and treatment-resistant, since it interferes earliest and most completely with retinal image formation during the critical period. Combined mechanisms (for example, strabismus together with anisometropia) are common and often produce deeper amblyopia than either mechanism alone.",
    urgency: 'Prompt',
    managementConsiderations:
      "Urgency is fundamentally age-dependent: visual system plasticity is greatest in infancy and early childhood and gradually declines through roughly age 8-10, though the Pediatric Eye Disease Investigator Group (PEDIG) Amblyopia Treatment Studies have shown that meaningful treatment benefit can still be achieved in some children and adolescents up to age 17, generally with a slower and less complete response than in younger children. Treating the underlying cause is the essential first step — full refractive correction for anisometropic or isoametropic amblyopia (which alone resolves a meaningful proportion of cases), cataract extraction for deprivation amblyopia, and appropriate strabismus management. Standard occlusion (patching) therapy of the sound/fellow eye, or pharmacologic penalization with atropine (shown to be similarly effective per the PEDIG ATS1 trial), is then dosed and monitored based on age, depth of amblyopia, and compliance, with closer monitoring in infants given the risk of iatrogenic occlusion amblyopia of the treated eye from excessive patching. A visually significant congenital cataract represents a true urgent-to-emergent surgical referral: the American Academy of Ophthalmology Preferred Practice Pattern on Amblyopia notes that visually significant unilateral congenital cataracts have a better visual prognosis when removed and optically corrected by about 2 months of age, reflecting how quickly early visual deprivation can produce profound, difficult-to-reverse amblyopia. Long-term maintenance therapy and monitoring for regression are continued through the visually immature years given the ongoing risk of recurrence after treatment is stopped.",
